SECTION 1 — NON-NEGOTIABLE OPERATING RULES

Rule 1: Brownfield-first analysis

This is an existing enterprise application.

Its current implementation, established architecture, coding patterns, domain logic, and integration contracts are the starting point.

Before recommending any agent:

- Explore the repository.
- Identify modules and dependencies.
- Trace the application architecture.
- Identify important execution flows.
- Understand backend integrations.
- Understand asynchronous processing.
- Understand resilience mechanisms.
- Understand testing strategies.
- Understand deployment and operational architecture.
- Identify recurring engineering difficulties.

Do not assume a particular architecture simply because the project uses Spring Boot.

Rule 2: Evidence-based conclusions

Every important architectural finding and agent recommendation must reference evidence from the repository.

Include:

- Relevant file paths.
- Class or interface names.
- Method names where applicable.
- Configuration properties.
- Supporting tests.
- Dependencies.
- Relevant execution flows.
- Architectural limitations.

Differentiate clearly between:

- CONFIRMED: Directly verified from code or configuration.
- INFERRED: Reasonable conclusion based on evidence.
- UNKNOWN: Not established from available repository content.
- RECOMMENDED: Proposed engineering improvement.

Never invent classes, endpoints, frameworks, or infrastructure.

Rule 3: Read-only discovery

During the initial analysis:

- Do not modify application source code.
- Do not refactor existing classes.
- Do not update dependencies.
- Do not change application properties.
- Do not change deployment configurations.
- Do not create custom agents yet.
- Do not install plugins or frameworks.
- Do not execute deployments.
- Do not connect to production systems.

Use safe, read-only inspection.

If running build or test commands would generate files or modify the environment, obtain approval first.

After completing discovery, present recommendations for review.

Only create agent configuration files after I explicitly approve the implementation plan.

Rule 4: Comprehensive repository coverage

Do not analyze only the currently open class.

Explore the entire accessible repository, including:

- Root and submodule build files.
- Java/Kotlin source directories.
- Resources and configuration.
- REST controllers.
- Service and orchestration layers.
- Backend connectors.
- GraphQL-related classes.
- SOAP clients.
- Messaging components.
- DTOs and domain models.
- Data mappers.
- Exception handling.
- Security configuration.
- Async/concurrency utilities.
- Resilience configurations.
- Unit and integration tests.
- CI/CD files.
- Kubernetes/OpenShift files.
- Existing developer documentation.
- Existing AI or Copilot configuration.

Inspect generated-source directories only when necessary.

Exclude irrelevant build outputs, binaries, dependency caches, and generated artifacts from detailed inspection.

If the repository is too large to examine in one session, analyze it in clearly defined batches and retain a coverage inventory.

Do not claim to have examined the whole repository unless coverage is demonstrated.

Rule 5: Protect enterprise information

Treat all repository material as potentially sensitive.

- Do not display or copy credentials, tokens, certificates, or private keys.
- Do not reproduce real patient/member information or protected health information.
- Do not send data to external services unless explicitly authorized.
- Do not modify secret-bearing configuration.
- Use synthetic examples.
- Recommend least-privilege tools and read-only access by default.
- Follow existing organizational policies.

